<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Tordesillas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Tordesillas was an agreement of 1494 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and Portugal that divided newly discovered lands outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> along a line of longitude west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> Islands. Lands to the west were assigned to Spain and those to the east to Portugal, which later allowed Portugal to claim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> treat it as a foundation of Spanish claims in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas.jpg" alt="Treaty of Tordesillas" width="306" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Biblioteca Nacional de Lisboa Photo: Joserebelo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treaty_of_Tordesillas.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The dispute arose after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> returned in 1493 from his first voyage under the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. He stopped first at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a>, and King John II of Portugal then claimed the new lands under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-alcacovas">Treaty of Alcáçovas</a> of 1479 and the papal bull Aeterni regis of 1481, which granted Portugal all lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a>. He wrote a threatening letter to Ferdinand and Isabella and prepared a fleet.</p><p>On 4 May 1493 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> decreed in the bull <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inter-caetera">Inter caetera</a> that lands west of a line 100 leagues west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azores">Azores</a> or the Cape Verde Islands should belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. John II felt this gave him too little: by 1493 Portuguese explorers had reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, and a later bull that named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> as Spanish was a major issue for his near-term goal. He therefore negotiated directly with the Catholic Monarchs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was signed in Tordesillas on 7 June 1494 and ratified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/setubal">Setúbal</a>. It placed the line of demarcation 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde Islands, with lands to the east for Portugal and to the west for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Portugal had asked for the line to be moved 270 leagues further west than the pope's, to protect its route along the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><p>The treaty did not define the league or say which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-verde">Cape Verde</a> island was meant. It provided that these points be settled by a joint voyage, which never took place, and later expert opinions placed the line at roughly 42° to 47° west. It was negotiated without consulting Alexander VI, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a> sanctioned it by the bull Ea quae pro bono pacis of 24 January 1506.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Very little of the divided area had been seen by Europeans. In 1500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-alvares-cabral">Pedro Álvares Cabral</a> landed on the easternmost part of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> on his way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and it fell to Portugal. Some historians contend that the Portuguese already knew of the coast. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> did not resist Portuguese expansion across the meridian in Brazil.</p><p>After Portugal reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maluku-islands">Moluccas</a> in 1512, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> argued that the treaty divided the whole globe in two. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zaragoza">Treaty of Zaragoza</a> of 22 April 1529 set an antimeridian and Spain gave up its claim to the Moluccas for 350,000 ducats of gold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-i">Francis I</a> and the Protestant maritime powers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, did not recognise a division of the world between two Catholic nations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-madrid-1750">Treaty of Madrid</a> of 1750 superseded the Tordesillas line, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> added the treaty to its Memory of the World register in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Tordesillas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Christopher Columbus under the Capitulations of Santa Fe in 1492, divided the non-European world with Portugal by the <b>Treaty of Tordesillas</b> in 1494, and treated the Americas as Castilian possessions: the Council of the Indies, founded in 1524, governed them under Castilian law, and Castilian Spanish became the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Tordesillas</b> of 1494, which divided the newly found lands with Castile, shaped the map of South America.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-portugal">Kingdom of Portugal · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The empire's legal and political foundations were set out in the papal bulls of 1493, above all Inter caetera, which assigned Spain the right to lands discovered to the west, and the <b>Treaty of Tordesillas</b> (1494), which fixed the meridian dividing the Spanish and Portuguese spheres.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Papal bulls such as Dum Diversas (1452) authorised the Portuguese king to subdue “Saracens and pagans” and reduce them to perpetual servitude, and after Christopher Columbus reached the Caribbean for Castile in 1492 the <b>Treaty of Tordesillas</b> (1494) divided the newly found lands between the two Iberian crowns.</blockquote><a href="/government/colonial-rule">Colonial rule · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Both had claimed these Spice Islands under the <b>Treaty of Tordesillas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-22">22 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Spain ratified the <b>Treaty of Tordesillas</b>, signed on 7 June, which divided newly discovered lands outside Europe between Portugal and Castile along a meridian 370 leagues west of the Cape Verde islands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-2">2 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
